 40#1644 40#1745 40#1846 40#1947 41#99 41#200 41#301 41#402 41#503 41#604 41#705 41#806 41#907 41#1008 41#1109 41#1210 41#1311 41#1412 41#1513 41#1614 41#1715 41#1816 41#1917 42#69 42#170 42#271 42#372 42#473 42#574 42#675 42#776 42#877 42#978 42#1079 42#1180 42#1281 42#1382 42#1483 42#1584 42#1685 42#1786 42#1887 42#1988 43#39 43#140 43#241 43#342 43#443 43#544 43#645 43#746 43#847 43#948 43#1049 43#1150 43#1251 43#1352 43#1453 43#1554 43#1655 43#1756 43#1857 43#1958 44#9 44#110 44#211 44#312 44#413 44#514 44#615 44#716 44#817 44#918 44#1019 44#1120 44#1221 44#1322 44#1423 44#1524 44#1625 44#1726 44#1827 44#1928 45#80 45#181 45#282 45#383 45#484 45#585 45#686 45#787 45#888 45#989 45#1090 45#1191 45#1292 45#1393 45#1494 45#1595 45#1696 45#1797 45#1898 45#1999 46#50 46#151 46#252 46#353 46#454 46#555 46#656 46#757 46#858 46#959 46#1060 46#1161 46#1262 46#1363 46#1464 46#1565 46#1666 46#1767 46#1868 46#1969 47#20 47#121 47#222 47#323 47#424 47#525 47#626 47#727 47#828 47#929 47#1030 47#1131 47#1232 47#1333 47#1434 47#1535 47#1636 47#1737 47#1838 47#1939 48#91 48#192 48#293 48#394 48#495 48#596 48#697 48#798 48#899 48#1000 48#1101 48#1202 48#1303 48#1404 48#1505 48#1606 48#1707 48#1808 48#1909 49#61 49#162 49#263 49#364 49#465 49#566 49#667 49#768 49#869 49#970 49#1071 49#1172 49#1273 49#1374 49#1475 49#1576 49#1677 49#1778 49#1879 49#1980 50#31 50#132 50#233 50#334 50#435 50#536 50#637 50#738 50#839 50#940 50#1041 50#1142 50#1243 50#1344 50#1445 50#1546 50#1647 50#1748 50#1849 50#1950

@@@ hint
Use a pointer to the link you might rewrite: `struct Timer **pp = &head;`. Advance it with `pp = &(*pp)->next` while the node it points to should stay **in front of** `t`.
@@@ hint
For stability, keep walking while `(*pp)->expiry <= t->expiry`; use `<=`, not `<`. Then `t->next = *pp; *pp = t; return head;`. Because `pp` started at `&head`, inserting at the front needs no special case.
@@@ solution_code
#include <stddef.h>

struct Timer *timer_insert(struct Timer *head, struct Timer *t)
{
    struct Timer **pp = &head;            /* address of the link we may rewrite */

    /* skip every node that must stay in front of t (<= keeps equal keys FIFO) */
    while (*pp != NULL && (*pp)->expiry <= t->expiry)
        pp = &(*pp)->next;

    t->next = *pp;                        /* splice t in front of *pp ... */
    *pp = t;                              /* ... works for head, middle and tail */
    return head;
}
@@@ explanation_md
**Approach.** Walk the list with a **pointer to a pointer**. `pp` holds the address of the link (`head` itself, or some node's `next`) that will point at `t`. Inserting at the head, in the middle or at the tail then becomes the same two assignments:

```
head -> [1#2] -> [3#4] -> [5#1] -> NULL       insert 5#3
pp: &head -> &n(1#2).next -> &n(3#4).next -> &n(5#1).next (5 <= 5, keep going)
t->next = NULL; n(5#1).next = t
```

The `<=` in the loop is what makes the insert **stable**. Two timers that expire on the same tick fire in the order they were started. Many RTOS kernels guarantee this for timers and for equal-priority ready lists.

**Complexity.** Time O(n) per insertion (O(n^2) to build a list of n). Space O(1).

**Common mistakes**
- Using `<` instead of `<=`. Equal expiries come out in reverse creation order (`7#4 7#3 7#2 7#1`).
- Special-casing the head with `if (t->expiry < head->expiry)` and getting the equal-key case wrong there.
- Forgetting to set `t->next` when appending at the tail. The harness sets it to `NULL`, but in real code a reused node still holds its old `next`, which creates a cycle.
- Comparing with subtraction (`a - b < 0`). With `INT_MIN`/`INT_MAX` that overflows, which is undefined behavior.

**Interview follow-ups**
- Tick counters wrap around. How do you compare expiries when `uint32_t` ticks overflow? (Compare `(int32_t)(a - b) < 0`.)
- Insert is O(n). What data structures give O(1) or O(log n) timer start? (Timer wheel, binary heap, delta list.)
- How would a **delta list** (each node stores the ticks after the previous node) change insert and the tick handler?
- This list is modified from task context and read from the tick ISR. What protection do you need?
